# Peer-dependency resolution is relaxed for ONE conflict, described below. Without
# this, `npm ci` fails outright in CI — and there are six separate `npm ci` calls
# across build/ci/deploy/sonar, so a flag on the command line is one workflow away
# from being forgotten. It belongs in the file every npm invocation reads.
#
# THE CONFLICT
#   @capacitor-firebase/authentication@7.5.0  declares  peerOptional firebase@^11.2.0
#   this project depends on                              firebase@^12.16.0
#
# WHY IT IS SAFE TO IGNORE, rather than something to actually fix:
#
#   That peer exists for the plugin's WEB implementation — the JS path that would
#   drive Firebase Auth in a browser. We never load it. src/features/auth/googleAuth.js
#   reaches the plugin through `registerPlugin('FirebaseAuthentication')` from
#   @capacitor/core, which talks straight to the native bridge; the package itself is
#   not imported anywhere in src/ (grep it — the only matches are comments). Web
#   sign-in uses the Firebase JS SDK directly at v12, entirely independent of the
#   plugin.
#
# WHY NOT THE ALTERNATIVES:
#
#   - Downgrading to firebase@11 would regress the SDK the whole web app and FCM use,
#     to satisfy a code path that never executes.
#   - Upgrading to @capacitor-firebase/authentication@8 requires Capacitor 8; this
#     project is on Capacitor 7 across all plugins.
#   - `overrides` in package.json is more targeted, but it only takes effect after
#     regenerating package-lock.json — which on Windows has previously stripped the
#     other platforms' optional native binaries and broken `npm ci` on Linux CI. The
#     current lockfile is verified intact (50 such entries); this setting needs no
#     lockfile change at all.
#
# IF YOU ADD A DEPENDENCY AND HIT A PEER WARNING, do not assume this line covers it.
# It suppresses the check globally, so a genuine conflict will also pass silently.
# Check the warning on its own merits first.
legacy-peer-deps=true
